Strategy commit is the next commit on top of the role-base.

## 1. Canonical-contract error being corrected

The previous strategy retry recorded the following false claim inside
`measure/tracks/apk_source_denominator_inventory_20260712/test-strategy.md`
under §"Role-map problem and infrastructure blocker":

> "The closest canonical role, `measure-adversarial-testing`, is documented
> as **audit-only** and routes exposed defects to Green rather than
> authoring acceptance artifacts."

That claim contradicts the canonical Measure Orchestrator skill
(`~/.agents/skills/measure-orchestrator/SKILL.md`) on two independent
grounds, both of which were misread in the prior strategy retry:

1. The SKILL §Delegation paragraph explicitly lists roles that "may edit
   within their explicit ownership": **"Red, Green, adversarial testing,
   orchestrator-audit guard-test work, and closeout may edit within their
   explicit ownership."** `measure-adversarial-testing` is named in that
   grant. The audit-only enumeration that follows the same paragraph
   includes only **"Review A/B/C, browser review, phase acceptance, and
   final acceptance"** — `measure-adversarial-testing` is not in that
   list.
2. The interphase checker
   (`~/.agents/skills/measure-orchestrator/scripts/measure_interphase_checks.py:41`)
   sets `CANNOT_COMMIT_ROLES = {"review-a", "review-b", "review-c",
   "phase-acceptance", "ux-browser-review", "final-acceptance"}`.
   `adversarial-testing` is **not** in that set, so its `role-gate`
   implementation does not gate `--role-base` or reject committed file
   deltas — it is permitted to commit within its explicit ownership.

The prior strategy retry read "audit-only" as a class label applied to
every audit-named canonical subagent, then over-generalized that class to
`measure-adversarial-testing`. That generalization is not supported by
either the SKILL text or the interphase implementation. It is a
strategy-owned canonical-contract error, not a project policy.

